@actorvijay
Today, around 5:00–5:30 PM, while visiting Kallazhagar Temple in Madurai, Tamil Nadu, I was stopped by a man who questioned me about taking photographs and videos inside the temple. 
When he instructed me to delete them, I immediately complied and showed him that I had deleted them.
However, despite my cooperation, he then took my phone from me without my permission and deleted other photographs from my device. 

When I asked him to identify himself and show his police ID, he did not provide any identification. Instead, he showed me a phone wallpaper depicting a gun in his hand, which I found extremely intimidating and threatening.

The person himself was not wearing a police uniform and did not show any official identification. Some other individuals accompanying him were wearing police uniforms, but their conduct towards me also made me feel threatened, even though I had already complied with the request and deleted the photographs and videos.

I am unable to understand how a visitor can be stopped, confronted, and have her personal phone taken and accessed without proper identification or explanation. Whatever the temple's rules regarding photography may be, they should be communicated respectfully and enforced through proper, lawful procedures.

As a woman and a visitor from another state, I felt extremely unsafe, intimidated, and disturbed by this incident. No visitor should have to experience such treatment at a place of worship.

I respectfully request the Hon’ble Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu @actorvijay and the concerned authorities to investigate this incident, verify the identities and official status of the persons involved, review the CCTV footage from the location around the relevant time, and take appropriate action if any misconduct or unauthorized conduct is established.

Tamil Nadu’s temples should be places of peace, safety, dignity, and respect for everyone—not places where visitors are made to feel threatened or intimidated.

